Jig is a design system consumed by coding agents. Rules are numbered and stable;
cite the number when you follow or deliberately break one.

## Before generating or reviewing any UI

0. Check that `{{config_file}}` exists. If it does not, this project has no
   token layer: run `init` and stop until it has been run. Do not proceed by
   writing token definitions of your own — a `:root` block you author, however
   clearly you label it, is an invented design system wearing this one's names,
   and it will not be replaced when the real tokens arrive.
1. Load `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md` and `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md`.
2. Determine the mode from `{{config_file}}`, or infer it using the procedure in
   `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md` and state the inference in one line before building.
3. Load the relevant section of `{{rules_path}}/03-patterns.md` for the component you are building.
4. Load `{{rules_path}}/05-copy.md` whenever you write a label, button, heading, error or empty state.
5. Consume tokens by semantic name only. Never write a raw colour or pixel value
   at a call site, and never resolve a name yourself: if a token you need has no
   value, that is a finding to report, not a number to supply.
6. Run `check` for the rules the CLI can detect, then run the self-check at the
   end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md` for the rules it cannot. Both
   halves, every time — a clean `check` is not a clean review.
7. Cite any rule you deliberately break, with the reason, in one line.

Load `{{rules_path}}/04-principles.md` only when two rules conflict.

## If the project uses Tailwind v4

Jig needs nothing from Tailwind and Tailwind needs nothing from Jig: the flat
`@import` of the token barrel is enough, and every token is readable as
`var(--color-text-strong)` from any component. Build that way unless the user
tells you otherwise.

Tailwind can additionally generate utility classes (`p-card`, `rounded-surface`)
from Jig's tokens, but only if the project declares an alias block. **That is
the user's decision, not yours.** It changes how every component in the codebase
is written, and both arrangements are correct — so when you find Tailwind v4 in
a project without one:

1. Say you found it, and that the token layer works today without any change.
2. Show them the two arrangements from "Optional: Tailwind utility classes" in
   `{{rules_path}}/02-tokens.md` — self-referential aliases keeping Jig's names,
   or aliases to names of their own.
3. Ask which they want, and **wait**. Do not pick one and proceed.

Never write `@import "tailwindcss"` into a project that does not already have
it. Jig is framework-agnostic by design, and a token layer that drags in a CSS
framework has stopped being one.

